  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 240 AM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot and humid conditions expected this week \npeaking with heat indices near 105 Wed and Thurs. Isolated storms \npossible, but mostly dry until late this week.  \n\nThe late summer heat wave is on track; temperatures in the upper 90s \nto around 100 are still expected over the next several days. A \nstrong UL ridge continues to build over the gulf and up to the Great \nLakes. This ridge will then translate east through the end of the \nweek, allowing temperatures to increase more. Highs will be in the \nmid 90s west of the bay and low 90s on the Eastern Shore. Heat \nindices will climb into the 100-103F range this afternoon. Highs \ntomorrow will be in the upper 90s for most and even touching 100 in \nthe southwest. A weak backdoor cold front pushing into the NE \ncomplicates the temperature forecast for the Eastern Shore and \nperhaps the Northern Neck. Onshore flow during the afternoon could \nkeep temps a bit cooler by comparison with highs around 90. \nWidespread heat indices around 105F west of the bay Wed are \nforecast. Heat Advisories appear likely. Similar conditions are \nexpected for Thurs with advisories once again likely. The heat \npotentially continues Friday, depending on the timing of a cold \nfront. Highest confidence in advisory conditions is therefore \nacross southern counties. \n\nRegarding thunderstorm chances, isolated convection is expected both \ntoday and tomorrow. The 00z CAMs as a whole are not all that excited \nabout either day, but kept in the slight chance in the NE today and \nfor most of the area tomorrow. Some of the CAMs and global models to \nindicate some stronger convection, perhaps in the form of linear \nsegments, to the north of the local FA. It is not outside the realm \nof possibility that those storms dive south into the local area. \nNorthwest flow aloft and strong instability means that any storms \nthat do form will have the potential for strong winds and large \nhail. SPC has maintained the Marginal Risk for NE portions of the \narea today and tomorrow. Tomorrow's slight risk does clip the MD\nEastern Shore, but this would be highly dependent on the\naforementioned stronger storms to the north diving south. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower/storm chances increase late week as a \ncold front approaches. Seasonable temps forecast behind the front.\n\nThe strong ridge aloft starts to break down/flatten late in the \nweek. Meanwhile, a sfc cold front will approach and pass through the \narea sometime Friday- likely late. Storms will be possible ahead of \nit Thurs evening. Chances continue Friday afternoon as the front \npushes south. NW flow will remain place aloft and progressively \nstrengthen as the associated trough drops southward along the NE/Mid-\nAtlantic coast. The combination of instability and this increasing \nflow (and shear) suggests any storms could be strong-severe. SPC has \noutlooked areas N of the NC/VA border in a severe risk for Thursday \nand Friday. \n\nWe should see a little bit of relief from the heat over the weekend, \nbut still seasonably warm with highs in the low 80s. Storm chances \ndecrease as strong high pressure builds in from the north.",
